    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35974</id>
    <title>How Different are Different Differences-in-Differences? A Large-Scale Meta-Analysis</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Peter Hull</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;I use AI-assisted replication to assess the empirical importance of negative weights in two-way fixed effects (TWFE) estimation, analyzing 1,430 staggered-adoption specifications from 116 economics papers. TWFE estimates are highly correlated with modern difference-in-differences estimates, with sign reversals in 12.0–14.9% of comparisons and greater precision for TWFE. Hierarchical models accounting for estimation noise imply estimand correlations of 91–97% and a 0.7–2.4% probability of a statistically significant TWFE estimate opposing the modern estimand’s sign. These findings suggest negative weights may be a less serious problem in practice and illustrate how AI can support systematic, large-scale empirical assessments of new econometric methods.</content>
