    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35979</id>
    <title>What Drives Censorship on College Campuses?</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Peter Arcidiacono, Cory Dowd &amp; Matthew Lilley</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;There has been widespread concern about self-censorship on university campuses, and especially that conservative voices are being stifled. Using survey data from a private elite college, we show that self-censorship is not simply about being conservative: it is students with the most moderate views who self-censor the most. This pattern is associated with confidence and salience — students who are more confident in their views or who see an issue as especially important tend to hold more extreme views and to self-censor less. At the same time, conservatives self-censor at higher rates than liberals, and the same individual is more likely to self-censor on the views where they are relatively more conservative than on those where they are relatively more liberal; conservatives are also far more likely than liberals to cite concerns about social exclusion as a reason for self-censoring, and report self-censoring less outside the university community. Groups perceived as marginalized self-censor less than majority groups, and especially so on issues salient to them (e.g., Black students on BLM).</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35979"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
