  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36035</id>
    <title>Will AI Growth Reach the Developing World?</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Daniel Björkegren, David Lagakos &amp; Mitchell VanVuren</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper explores how economic growth driven by AI will impact the developing world. Our analysis is based on a growth model of a developed and developing economy where AI services are traded on the world market, and may substitute for or complement different labor inputs in production. Preferences allow for the possibility that demand for services provided by AI has an upper bound. The model predicts that developing countries may not benefit as much from AI-driven growth. First, they may lack complementary skills needed to fully utilize AI domestically. Second, they may be priced out of world markets due to their relatively lower productivity. We use our model to simulate how growth and welfare are affected by making workers more complementary to AI, subsidizing compute, speeding technology diffusion, and providing cash transfers. Numerical simulations of the model suggest that the potential for developing countries to be left behind – or having some workers lose in absolute terms – is substantial enough that researchers should study the impacts of AI on developing countries in real time.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36035"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
