  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36034</id>
    <title>The Recent and Impending Evolution of US Federal Support for State and Local Governments</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jeffrey Clemens &amp; Anwita Mahajan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;In the US federal system, the federal government provides substantial fiscal support to state and local governments. In this paper, we describe how the landscape of intergovernmental grants, which exceeded $1.2 trillion in 2025, evolved during and in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic. We provide short-run evidence on how state and local government budgets have adapted as pandemic-era fiscal supports wound down. We further emphasize the pressures state and local budgets will face as several provisions of the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (OBBBA), which are intended to restrain intergovernmental grants, go into effect over the coming years.</content>
