  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36033</id>
    <title>Preconception Medicaid and Maternal and Infant Health: Evidence from Linked Administrative Data</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Genna Campain, Sarah Miller, Petra Persson, Maya Rossin-Slater, Laura Wherry &amp; Katlyn N. King</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Despite pregnancy-focused public insurance coverage in the U.S., low-income mothers and infants face persistent health disadvantages. Using linked birth, tax, and enrollment data, we examine the effects of preconception coverage from California's 2014 Medicaid expansion, comparing changes in outcomes for first-time mothers just below and above the income eligibility threshold. Expansion increased preconception Medicaid enrollment by 3.4 months (+23 percentage points in 12-month continuous coverage), first-month pregnancy enrollment by 24 percentage points, and adequate prenatal care and STI screening. Yet we find no improvements in maternal or infant health, suggesting preconception coverage alone cannot close health disparities.</content>
