  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36032</id>
    <title>Clouds of Credit: Mapping the Financial Web Behind AI Compute</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Stijn Van Nieuwerburgh &amp; Zijin Su</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We map the financial network supporting the AI buildout. Substantial equity cushions protect creditors against large obligations outstanding in mid-2026, but concentrated exposures and interconnected claims create weak links. Our calibrated model supports a large compute expansion in the coming years. The equilibrium investment exceeds the social optimum because firms do not internalize their effects on competitors' revenues. Debt finances nearly 60% of the expansion, increasing leverage, while non-recourse borrowing leaves project lenders exposed despite equity buffers. In the low-productivity state, losses reach $16.8 trillion across shareholders and creditors, with 40% loss-given-default on debt. Suppressing contractual payment spillovers among the key firms reduces private losses by approximately 30%, revealing substantial network propagation.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36032"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
