  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36036</id>
    <title>How Does Capital Flow To Poor Countries? Evidence from Venture Capital and Private Equity</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Emanuele Colonnelli, Marcio Cruz, Josh Lerner, Mariana Pereira-Lopez &amp; Yuanchang Wei</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Why does so little private capital flow to poor countries? We study how venture capital and private equity investors evaluate firms across countries, using an experimental survey of 1,485 investors in 108 countries who are incentivized to evaluate hypothetical investment opportunities with randomized firm characteristics, locations, and investment terms. Investors respond similarly to firm fundamentals, but otherwise comparable firms attract substantially less interest in poorer countries. This poor-country penalty is much smaller among investors specialized in emerging markets. Detailed survey data show that emerging market and developed market investors have largely similar investment practices, but differ in how they obtain country information, assess and manage country risks, and exit investments. Investors reporting active country-risk management apply smaller country penalties, even after accounting for market focus. Our findings suggest that investors' specialization and investment practices shape how country risk affects investment: the same firm can face drastically different financing prospects depending on where it is located and which investors evaluate it.</content>
