  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36028</id>
    <title>Crash Injuries and Fatalities in the United States Following Standardization of Automatic Emergency Braking</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Vishal Patel, Christopher M. Worsham, Michael Liu-Hass, Thomas Tsai &amp; Anupam Jena</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Whether vehicle safety technologies reduce crash injuries and fatalities is relevant to consumers and regulators, but rigorous real world evidence is limited because drivers who choose optional features differ from those who do not. As a natural experiment, we leveraged the staggered implementation of automatic emergency braking as standard rather than optional equipment on 39 vehicle models between 2017 and 2022. Across the United States, the percentage of crash injuries from rear-end collisions caused by the vehicle fell from 13.9% to 8.0% at the first standard model year, and the percentage of crash fatalities fell from 6.0% to 4.7%. Neither fell in models that never standardized the technology, and crash types it was not designed to prevent were unaffected. Automatic emergency braking reduces crash injuries and fatalities.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36028"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
