  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36026</id>
    <title>Crime on ICE: Local Police Behavior and Immigration Enforcement in the United States</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>John J. Donohue, Tai Markman &amp; Eric A. Baldwin</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;A growing number of local police departments now hold authority to enforce federal immigration law, a role traditionally reserved for federal officers. Despite claims that this authority enhances public safety, empirical evidence remains limited. Using FBI Uniform Crime Report data from 2000–2024, we exploit staggered adoption across U.S. jurisdictions to estimate causal effects on policing. We find that this enforcement authority increases arrests for low-level, discretionary offenses by approximately 7%, with no corresponding decreases in offense rates or serious crime. These results suggest that such agreements realign police priorities toward discretionary enforcement without measurable public safety benefits.</content>
