  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36025</id>
    <title>The Health Effects of Eating Red Meat: A Natural Experiment in Alpha-Gal Syndrome</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Vishal Patel, Christopher M. Worsham, Michael Liu-Hass &amp; Anupam Jena</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Observational associations between red meat consumption and cardiometabolic disease are confounded by diet and other health behaviors. Alpha-gal syndrome, a tick-borne allergy to mammalian meat, leads to an abrupt and involuntary cessation of red meat consumption. Using national electronic health records, we compared changes in weight and cardiometabolic health over two years before and after newly detected alpha-gal syndrome with those in patients with other tick-borne diseases, who were also bitten and sought care but whose diagnoses carry no dietary restriction. Relative to this comparison group, diagnosis of alpha-gal syndrome led to a sustained 0.8 kg (95% CI, 0.6 to 1.0) reduction in body weight that increased with the degree of sensitization, as well as small reductions in triglycerides, glycated hemoglobin, glucose, and low-density lipoprotein cholesterol. Not eating red meat appears to yield modest but measurable cardiometabolic benefits.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36025"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
