    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36015</id>
    <title>Education and Gender Voting Polarization: The Effect of Compulsory Schooling on Sex-Based Preferences for the Democratic and Republican Parties</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Philip DeCicca, Harry Krashinsky &amp; Erik T. Nesson</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;One of the most notable developments in U.S. electoral politics over the last 40 years is the increasing divide between men and women voters.  While men and women had roughly equivalent levels of support for both the Democratic and Republican parties in 1980, more recent surveys have revealed a roughly 10 to 15-percentage-point gap in preference for the Democratic party among women relative to men.  We explain this result by using a variety of econometric strategies to establish a novel connection between compulsory schooling laws (CSLs), educational attainment, and diverging political preferences by sex.  First, we show that the timing of this divergence is coincident with recent changes in CSLs, and that this divergence is almost entirely due to changes in sex-related preferences for the Democratic party in states where there exists a much stronger effect of CSLs on average educational attainment -- very little change in sex-related preferences for the Democratic party is present in other states.  Furthermore, in states with more-impactful CSLs, changes in CSLs and their associated increased educational attainment explain almost all of the diverging support for political parties and differences in opinion over major policy issues by sex, such as gun control and abortion.  Altogether, these new facts present an original explanation that relies upon the effect of education to explain the evolution of gender political preferences in the U.S. over the last 40 years.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36015"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
