  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36014</id>
    <title>The Labor Market Impact of Temporary High-Skill Immigration Programs</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>George J. Borjas</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;In 2019, about 1.2 million foreign workers were employed through temporary high-skill programs, including H-1B and OPT. This paper identifies likely temporary workers in the American Community Survey and measures their supply by education and field of degree. Natives in the most exposed skill groups experienced slower earnings growth and a relative decline in full-time year-round work. Many temporary workers stay after their status ends, but surveys cannot identify these stayers. A model of temporary inflows shows that, under specified conditions, the coefficient from an IV regression of native outcomes on the number of current temporary workers embodies a stayer multiplier, capturing the effects of both those workers and earlier stayers. Multiplying the coefficient by the observed number of temporary workers recovers the programs’ full effect without data on stayers. The estimates imply that natives with science and engineering degrees earn about 3.3 percent less, and those with master’s degrees in computing or electrical and computer engineering 15 to 16 percent less. With constant returns, long-run capital adjustment, and no externalities, the estimated wage losses imply production complementarities that raised average wages by about 1.3 percent.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36014"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
