    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36020</id>
    <title>The Effects of Years of Health Insurance on the Health of Older Adults</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Yu Liu, Kevin Callison &amp; Robert Kaestner</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Whether longer exposure to health insurance improves health remains an open empirical question. This study examines the health effects of an additional year of health insurance coverage by exploiting differential changes in coverage pre-to-post implementation of the Affordable Care Act (ACA) for those who were and were not insured prior to the ACA. Using longitudinal data from the Health and Retirement Study (HRS), we instrument for insurance duration using baseline uninsured status interacted with the post-ACA period. We find that the ACA increased insurance coverage by approximately one additional year among previously uninsured respondents relative to those insured at baseline. Instrumental-variable estimates provide no statistically significant evidence of improvements in self-rated health, depressive symptoms, or hypertension over a four-year follow-up period. These findings are robust across alternative specifications. Overall, results provide little evidence that longer insurance coverage translates into measurable short-term health improvements, while leaving open the possibility of modest or longer-term benefits.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36020"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
