    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36008</id>
    <title>Monetary policy in a Phelps–Winter Economy</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Bruce C. Greenwald &amp; Joseph E. Stiglitz</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We use the Phelps-Winter customer market—providing a better theory of price determination for large parts of the economy than the standard model—to show that raising interest rates induces higher prices, as firms value current profits more, and are more willing to lose customers for short-term gains. These inflationary effects can be significant in relevant ranges of interest rates; they exist in the short run even when interest rate hikes succeed in cutting aggregate demand; and that they are amplified by policy uncertainty and the uncertainty about the effects of interest rate changes, both of which have increased as conventional policy has demonstrably failed badly to keep inflation at the 2% target. Empirical studies confirm this low level of ineffectiveness of raising interest rates in reducing inflation.  The weakening effectiveness of monetary policy was predicted by our earlier work on banks and the supply of credit, as a result of the elimination of interest prohibition on deposits; and further suggested by work on the structural transformation of the economy, as customer markets become more important.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36008"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
