  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36012</id>
    <title>Last-Mile Delivery and the Future of Foreign Aid</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Erika Deserranno, Andrea Guariso &amp; Nancy Qian</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Foreign aid seeks to provide basic services, such as health care and education, to poor and vulnerable populations. Its effectiveness depends on last-mile delivery: whether those services reach the people who need them. This article reviews evidence on two key aspects of last-mile delivery. The first concerns the growing role of non-state providers, especially NGOs, and its implications for the state’s capacity to deliver services and coordinate across providers. The second concerns the selection, motivation, and management of frontline workers who provide the services directly to beneficiaries. We argue that decisions about aid allocation and withdrawal should account for the need to preserve last-mile delivery capacity, which takes time and resources to build but can quickly erode.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36012"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
