  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36011</id>
    <title>Investment and Governance: Through The Lens of Sustainability</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jitendra Aswani &amp; Roberto Rigobon</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Using 3,944 sustainable bond issuances by public firms from 2013 to 2022, we examine the causal effect of sustainable investment on governance adoption. A heteroskedasticity-based identification strategy reveals that a 1% increase in sustainable debt (by total debt) raises the likelihood of sustainable governance adoption by 9.2%. Such adoption improves environmental performance by 16% (structural estimates). These effects are confined to non-financial firms; for financial firms, governance adoption alone is in-sufficient, as sustainable loan borrowers environmental performance does not improve. Firms with stronger environmental performance are less likely to adopt sustainable governance. Results are robust to alternative definitions and extensive specification checks.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36011"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
