    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36060</id>
    <title>Light Pollution: Effects on Sleep, Work, and Mental Health</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Joan Costa-i-Font, Janet Currie &amp; Soodeh Saberian</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This study examines the impact of artificial streetlights on sleep, as well as downstream effects on mental health, absenteeism, and physical activity. We exploit variation resulting from the replacement of traditional sodium streetlights with light-emitting diode (LED) lights in Burnaby, British Columbia, Canada. Using data from the Canadian Community Health Survey, we examine discontinuities in difficulty sleeping at the municipal border with Vancouver, which did not adopt new lighting.  We also estimate a within-Burnaby model exploiting the rollout of LED lights across neighborhoods, and a difference-in-differences design using matched Vancouver and Burnaby neighborhoods.  The estimates show that conversion reduces difficulty sleeping immediately, and that improvements in mental health, absenteeism, and physical activity follow with a lag of two to three years.  The largest gains occur with warmer-toned LEDs that more closely mimic natural light.</content>
