    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36050</id>
    <title>Past Automation and Future AI: How Weak Links Tame the Growth Explosion</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Charles I. Jones &amp; Christopher Tonetti</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;How much past growth is due to automation, and what does this imply for the future of AI? We perform task-based growth accounting and show that capital task productivity has grown about 5 percentage points per year faster than labor task productivity in the U.S. economy. The main benefit of automation is using rapidly improving machines instead of slowly-improving humans on more tasks. With these historical measures, we calibrate a growth model in which the production of goods and ideas is endogenously automated. Growth accelerates and can explode. However, the acceleration is slowed by "weak links," tasks still performed by labor.</content>
