    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36049</id>
    <title>Bureaucratic Rigidity and Battle Outcomes: Evidence from Military Logistics in the Ming Dynasty</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jingjing Chen, Jianlei Han, Shing-Yi Wang &amp; Yongxiang Wang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Bureaucratic rules meant to improve accountability can undermine state effectiveness. We study a 1492 Ming dynasty reform that shifted military grain delivery from salt merchants to a state relay system with prefecture-to-prefecture handovers and fixed routes. Afterward, court records document more supply shortages at formerly merchant-supplied garrisons than at state-supplied ones. Using 406 defensive battles against foreign invaders, we find the reform reduced victory rates near these garrisons. Declines were largest where resupply mattered most (longer wars, walled-city defenses) and where the state’s rules were most costly (routes crossing more prefectures or exposed to floods, droughts, or rugged terrain).</content>
