    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36048</id>
    <title>Are Revealed Preferences All You Need for AI Alignment?</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Joshua S. Gans &amp; Richard T. Holden</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;AI agents learn what principals want from what principals choose. We offer a criterion for when a principal's decisions, at every belief an AI might hold, are all the AI needs. For acting, they are. Cautious actions, such as deferring to a person, typically divide the principal's actions into blocks, and for choosing costless evidence, decisions are enough exactly when the same evidence is best in every block. Otherwise, the AI acts as its principal would yet can gather the wrong evidence, and greater capability can turn that error into incorrect actions. Choices between two mistakes reveal what decisions miss.</content>
