    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36046</id>
    <title>Valuing the Cost of Incomplete Global Markets</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Karen K. Lewis &amp; Edith X. Liu</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Deviations from complete financial markets in cross-border investments generate economic welfare costs. This paper shows how to value these costs using standard theory and data based on three main results. Our first result shows how welfare valuations can be derived from a general wealth-to-dividend ratio. Next, we establish how aggregate consumption and exchange rate data can be used to uncover diversifiable goods allocations across countries. Finally, we demonstrate how asset pricing data and models can be used to value deviations from complete markets. The paper outlines a range of applications that fall under this framework, including financial intermediary constraint models.</content>
