  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36041</id>
    <title>Does Treating Addiction Rebuild Lives? Evidence from Buprenorphine</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Sarah Eichmeyer, Aljoscha Janssen &amp; Jonathan Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Does treating addiction improve economic circumstances as well as health? Using two decades of Veterans Health Administration records on over 140,000 patients with opioid use disorder and consumer credit data, we exploit staggered facility-level buprenorphine adoption and quasi-random emergency physician assignment. A five-percentage-point increase in the share of clinicians with buprenorphine prescribing experience doubles overdose survivors’ long-term medication use and reduces two-year mortality by an estimated 3.7 percentage points. Instrumenting receipt yields a 22 percent credit score gain. At $300,000 per statistical life-year, estimated survival benefits alone exceed added medical costs within three years</content>
