    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36040</id>
    <title>The Emergence of a National Capital Market,1870–1940</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Pawel Janas</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This article documents the integration of the American capital market with the first long-run, geolocated, firm-level record of the asset holdings of large institutional investors: over 100,000 holdings of life insurers, 1870–1940. Between 1880 and 1890 the mean distance of insurers’ bonds from home more than doubled and their home-state share fell from over half to under a quarter. The shift occurred within firms, across bond classes and charters. Mortgage lending was national wherever state law permitted it. Integration was largely complete by 1890.</content>
