    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36039</id>
    <title>Democratizing Venture Capital: Tax Incentives and the Geographic Expansion of Startup Investing</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Murillo Campello &amp; Guilherme Junqueira</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Can tax subsidies expand the geographic reach of startup investing? Despite decades of growth, venture capital (VC) remains concentrated in a handful of metropolitan areas, leaving most of the country with limited access to startup financing. We study the 2009–2010 enhancement of the Qualified Small Business Stock (QSBS) program, which eliminated federal capital gains taxes on investments in qualifying startups. By raising the payoff to success, the reform made riskier investments more attractive, including entry into unfamiliar markets. This incentive is especially strong for VC funds. Relative to similarly sized metros that already had VC, the reform raised the probability that a previously unserved metro received VC investment in qualifying sectors by 8 to 10 percentage points. There was no comparable increase in sectors excluded from QSBS or among angel investors, who receive the same tax benefit. Startups in newly served markets performed like those in other non-hub markets, created high-wage jobs, and attracted scientific and technical workers, some from established hubs. Our findings suggest that a sufficiently large subsidy can extend VC into markets it had long bypassed, even with no requirement on where startups are located.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36039"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
