  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36038</id>
    <title>Five Facts About Stablecoins</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Lin William Cong, Gordon Y. Liao, Eswar S. Prasad &amp; Tony Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We document five facts about payment stablecoins using wallet-level blockchain data from 2020 to 2025. First, stablecoins serve as a transaction currency: onchain volume exceeds Bitcoin’s despite a much smaller supply, velocity has risen sharply, and transactions have shifted toward low-value transfers. Second, using transaction timing to refine exchange-based attribution, we find activity spread across the Americas, Europe, and Asia and fewer cross-regional flows than the standard method implies. Third, we estimate a structural cash-management model on the holdings data and find that the costs of accessing stablecoin wallets have fallen since 2023. Fourth, stablecoins are becoming the settlement currency of a fast-growing onchain credit market, and their growth appears to reallocate rather than destroy credit. Fifth, smart contracts are involved in about a third of Ethereum stablecoin transactions and over two thirds of volume, and protocols such as x402 demonstrate sub-cent machine-to-machine payments. Stablecoins behave as tokenized money, with implications for financial markets and regulation.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36038"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
