  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36037</id>
    <title>Designing Low Emission Zones: Theory and Evidence</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Nano Barahona, Natalia Fabra, Imelda &amp; Juan-Pablo Montero</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Low Emission Zones (LEZs), which exclude polluting vehicles from parts of a city, are spreading. Yet how their design affects welfare is poorly understood. Unlike price instruments, LEZs ration access inefficiently, imposing costs on inframarginal drivers of restricted vehicles. In our model, welfare is quasi-convex in LEZ size, yielding bang-bang principles: LEZs should be as large as possible or not implemented at all; cleaner vehicles (e.g., hybrids) could be exempt as transition technologies but later restricted, which may require planner commitment. Using new-car purchases, we find Madrid’s LEZ shifted buyers toward cleaner cars, but the surplus losses of switchers and inframarginal drivers exceeded emission savings.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36037"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
