  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36058</id>
    <title>Beyond the First Best: Welfare and OPEC Market Power</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>John Asker, Allan Collard-Wexler, Charlotte De Cannière &amp; Jan De Loecker</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We introduce an empirical framework to quantify the welfare impact of market power, through coordinated production decisions by a cartel, while allowing for other distortions. We contrast two novel concepts – the marginal and the infra-marginal welfare loss – to a naive approach that fails to recognize the existence of other distortions operating alongside market power. We apply the framework to measure the impact of market power of OPEC in the global crude oil market. We leverage unique micro data on cost and production to quantify the deadweight loss and productive inefficiency due to the OPEC cartel. From 1970 onward, the naive approach implies that OPEC generated 2.2 trillion dollars of welfare losses, two-thirds from productive inefficiency and one-third from deadweight loss. Eliminating cost-minimization violations outside the cartel shrinks losses to around 0.7 trillion dollars, indicating that other frictions in the oil market are complementary to OPEC’s market power.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36058"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
