    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36054</id>
    <title>Mortality Effects of the 2025 US Aid Freeze: Evidence from Zambia</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Torsten Figueiredo Walter, Dean Karlan, Niclas Moneke, Ana Radu &amp; Khozya Zyambo</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study the impact of the 2025 sudden freeze then subsequent thaw of US foreign aid on HIV prevention, treatment, infection, and death. In Zambia, HIV-related aid was administered through two agencies. Activities under one were disrupted for several months, and those under the other much less. Comprehensive data from all Zambian health facilities reveal immediate differential reductions in preventive HIV measures that mostly later recovered, followed by more HIV-positive pregnant women, more HIV-exposed births, more opportunistic infections, and more recorded deaths. For example, after one year we observe a 14% [95% CI: 3, 25] increase in HIV-positive pregnant women and a 26% [95% CI: 8, 45] increase in deaths among people living with HIV who were receiving antiretroviral therapy.</content>
